When and What to Prune? Stage-Aware Visual Token Pruning for Efficient VLA Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! Learn more × Back to arXiv License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.05273v1 [cs.CV] 04 Oct 2026 When and What to Prune? Stage-Aware Visual Token Pruning for Efficient VLA Tianjun Shi Affiliation: University of Macau Email: mc55413@um.edu.mo Haotian Xiong Affiliation: University of Macau Email: mc55441@um.edu.mo Ziyu Gong Affiliation: University of Macau Email: mc55143@um.edu.mo Qi Lu Affiliation: University of Macau Email: yc57960@um.edu.mo Li Li † † thanks: Corresponding author. Affiliation: University of Macau Email: llili@um.edu.mo Abstract Visual token pruning is an effective way to accelerate vision-language models and is especially useful for vision-language-action (VLA) inference, where many visual tokens must be processed before predicting robot actions. Existing pruning methods usually estimate which tokens can be pruned based on attention scores or feature diversity, retaining tokens that are either highly attended or visually different from others. However, most of them use fixed pruning schedules, such as pruning once at a preset layer or pruning at uniformly spaced layers. Such schedules can be risky for VLA models, because the model may not know which visual regions matter for the action in early layers. Tokens that look unimportant at first may become useful after the model combines visual observations with the language instruction. In this work, we propose SAPrune , a training-free visual token pruning framework for efficient VLA inference. Instead of pruning at fixed layers, SAPrune uses a small calibration set to observe how action-to-visual attention changes across layers, and chooses pruning layers only after the attention pattern becomes more reliable. At each selected layer, SAPrune applies a dual-path pruning rule: one path protects strongly attended visual tokens from pruning, while the other prevents useful surrounding context from being discarded. Experiments on LIBERO, SIMPLER, and real-world robotic tasks show that SAPrune prunes 87.5% of visual tokens and achieves up to 1.718 × 	imes inference speedup while maintaining competitive task success rates. 1 Introduction Vision-language-action (VLA) models have recently shown strong potential for embodied decision making by mapping visual observations and language instructions directly to robot actions [ 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 ] . Despite their effectiveness, VLA inference remains computationally expensive because a large number of visual tokens must be propagated through deep Transformer layers [ 5 , 7 , 8 , 9 ] . Visual token pruning is therefore a natural direction for reducing latency and computation, as shown in prior studies on efficient Vision Transformers and large vision-language models [ 10 , 11 , 12 , 13 , 14 , 15 , 16 , 17 ] . Figure 1 : Layer-wise visualization of attention in VLA. Although recent studies have shown substantial redundancy in VLA inference, most pruning-oriented acceleration methods still decide token removal from local importance cues, visual saliency, feature-space diversity, or their fixed combinations [ 13 , 15 , 14 , 16 , 18 , 17 , 8 , 9 ] . This treats pruning mainly as a static token-selection problem. Such a formulation is insufficient for action generation, where visual evidence does not remain fixed across depth: it gradually emerges, redistributes, and stabilizes as the model forms an action decision. Moreover, visual distinctiveness alone does not imply decision usefulness, since tokens that are geometrically diverse may still be irrelevant to the current action. These limitations motivate us to reconsider VLA pruning from two questions: when pruning becomes reliable, and what action-relevant evidence should be preserved. To understand when visual tokens can be safely pruned during VLA inference, we analyze the attention from action-related states to visual tokens across Transformer layers. Figure 1 shows that early layers attend to a broad set of visual tokens, while middle and deep layers gradually concentrate on tokens related to the target action. Figure 2(a) further quantifies this trend using the top- k k attention mass: the mass increases quickly from shallow to middle layers and then changes more slowly. This suggests that early layers have not yet clearly determined which visual tokens are useful for the action. Therefore, pruning too early may remove tokens that become important later, motivating us to delay pruning until the attention pattern becomes more stable. We further compare different pruning criteria in Fig. 2(b) . Attention-only selection tends to concentrate on dominant regions and may miss supportive context, whereas feature-space diversity-based selection may retain tokens that are visually distinctive but weakly related to the current action. These observations suggest that attention-only selection, feature-space diversity-based selection, and their fixed combination are all insufficient for aggressive VLA pruning. (a) Visualization of Top-k Importance Image Token Received Attention Ratio. (b) Comparison of Performance under Different Token Pruning Strategies. Figure 2 : Preliminary observations on top-k attention concentration and token pruning strategy comparison. Based on these principles, we propose SAPrune , a training-free visual token pruning framework for efficient VLA inference. SAPrune first selects pruning layers from rollout-level action-attention dynamics, and then applies a dual-path retention mechanism at each selected layer. The attention-core path preserves directly action-relevant tokens, while the functional-diversity path selects complementary tokens using query-conditioned functional signatures. Experiments on LIBERO [ 19 ] , SIMPLER [ 20 ] , and real-world robotic tasks show that SAPrune retains only 32 visual tokens, prunes 87.5% of the original visual tokens, and achieves up to 1.718 × 	imes speedup while maintaining competitive task success rates. 2 Preliminary 2.1 Related Works Efficient inference for VLA models. Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models extend large vision-language models by introducing an action modality, enabling robots to predict executable actions from visual observations and language instructions. Representative generalist robot policies include RT-1 [ 1 ] , RT-2 [ 2 ] , Open X-Embodiment / RT-X [ 3 ] , Octo [ 4 ] , OpenVLA [ 5 ] , π 0 \pi_{0} [ 6 ] . Although effective for general-purpose manipulation, these models repeatedly process dense visual tokens through deep Transformer layers, making inference costly for real-time robot deployment [ 7 , 8 , 21 , 22 , 9 ] . Visual token pruning for VLMs. Visual token pruning reduces visual redundancy by retaining a compact subset of informative visual tokens. Early efficient Vision Transformer methods, such as DynamicViT [ 10 ] , EViT [ 11 ] , and ToMe [ 12 ] , reduce computation through token sparsification or merging. For large vision-language models, attention-based methods such as FastV [ 13 ] , SparseVLM [ 15 ] , FoPru [ 23 ] , HiPrune [ 24 ] , LVPruning [ 16 ] , IVTP [ 14 ] , and ZSPA-Prune [ 25 ] rank tokens by prefill attention, text-to-vision attention, or prompt-conditioned relevance. Other methods preserve diversity or global representativeness [ 17 , 18 , 26 , 27 ] , while calibration or layer-adaptive methods determine pruning layers, ratios, or progressive schedules from calibration statistics or cross-layer information flow [ 28 , 29 , 30 , 31 , 32 ] . However, these methods are mainly designed for VLM understanding and do not explicitly model action-dependent visual evidence in VLA inference. Training-free acceleration for VLA models. Training-free VLA acceleration mainly follows temporal reuse, adaptive computation, and pruning-based acceleration. VLA-Cache [ 7 ] reuses visual features across adjacent timesteps, while VLA-ADP [ 22 ] dynamically reduces computation during manipulation. EfficientVLA [ 8 ] , SP-VLA [ 21 ] , SpecPrune-VLA [ 33 ] , and VLA-Pruner [ 9 ] reduce visual-token computation during VLA inference. These methods reveal substantial redundancy in VLA models, but often rely on fixed pruning locations, semantic relevance, temporal reuse, or additional acceleration modules. They leave underexplored how to choose pruning layers and preserve action-relevant evidence under aggressive token reduction. 2.2 VLA Inference and Action-to-Visual Attention VLA inference. Vision-language-action (VLA) models map visual observations and language instructions to executable robot actions. At rollout step t t , the model takes an image observation I t I_{t} and a language instruction x t x_{t} as input, and predicts an action a t a_{t} . For notational simplicity, we omit the timestep index in the following. The vision encoder produces N N visual tokens V ( 0 ) = { v 1 ( 0 ) , … , v N ( 0 ) } V^{(0)}=\{v^{(0)}_{1},\ldots,v^{(0)}_{N}\} , while the text encoder produces M M text tokens X ( 0 ) = { x 1 ( 0 ) , … , x M ( 0 ) } X^{(0)}=\{x^{(0)}_{1},\ldots,x^{(0)}_{M}\} . Depending on the backbone, action-related states can be implemented as action tokens, action placeholders, or action query states, denoted by Q a ( 0 ) Q^{(0)}_{a} , as in OpenVLA [ 5 ] , π 0 \pi_{0} [ 6 ] . The initial multimodal sequence is H ( 0 ) = V ( 0 ) ⊕ X ( 0 ) ⊕ Q a ( 0 ) , H^{(0)}=V^{(0)}\oplus X^{(0)}\oplus Q^{(0)}_{a}, (1) where ⊕ \oplus denotes sequence concatenation. The sequence is then propagated through L L Transformer layers: H ( l ) = F ( l ) ​ ( H ( l − 1 ) ) , H ( l ) = V ( l ) ⊕ X ( l ) ⊕ Q a ( l ) . H^{(l)}=F^{(l)}(H^{(l-1)}),\qquad H^{(l)}=V^{(l)}\oplus X^{(l)}\oplus Q^{(l)}_{a}. (2) The final robot action is predicted from the action-related output states. Since visual tokens usually occupy a large portion of the multimodal sequence, reducing V ( l ) V^{(l)} is an effective way to lower VLA infe